      "writtenOn": "2025-02-21T20:12:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah I originally meant that but then I didn\u0027t want to give two separate meanings to the single annotation.\n\nNow @RavenwoodPartiallyAllowlisted just tells the reader (of the code) that you can\u0027t use annotations freely in this class, and we don\u0027t actually change what actual policies are in effect.\n -- usually @KeepPartialClass on the same class, but maybe we just have annotations on nested classes. We may also have @Ridrect.\n \nI think this is easier to explain to other people, than \"oh this RavenwoodPartiallyAllowlisted means XXX _and_ also YYY\".\n\n@RavenwoodPartiallyAllowlisted is also in a way redundant, because it wouldn\u0027t work without `allow-annotation` in the policy file, and if we have it in the policy file, we don\u0027t _really_ need @RavenwoodPartiallyAllowlisted. But again, this is purely a marker to warn the code maintainer they can\u0027t freely expose more APIs. (without it, you\u0027d have to carefully check the policy files to see if that\u0027s the case.)",
